//? Title: Random variate generation: actual raw words, rejection and cached normal draws
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Simulation needs reproducible observations with specified distributions, not
just a stream of raw integer words. Canonical datamunge samplers transform
SplitMix64 output into bounded integers, Bernoulli outcomes, normal observations
and exponential waiting times. Actual raw offsets expose rejection and the
normal spare cache. The original seed0xC0FFEE,10,000 interleaved tuples and
final uniform draw remain; fixed-seed summaries are descriptive simulation
results.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

The uniform real sampler maps the high53 bits to \(u=k/2^{53}\in[0,1)\). For an
inclusive integer interval of width s, multiply the full raw word x by s, reject
low products below \(2^{64}\bmod s\), and return the accepted high word plus the
lower endpoint. This removes the unequal remainder cells without relying on weak
low bits; the full uint64 interval returns the raw word directly.
$$ B=\mathbf1\{u<p\},\quad E=-\log(1-u)/\lambda,\quad (Z_1,Z_2)=\sqrt{-2\log u_1}\,(\cos2\pi u_2,\sin2\pi u_2). $$
\alttext(Bernoulli thresholding, exponential inverse transformation and the two-output Box-Muller normal transform.)
The normal sampler retries a zero first uniform draw and caches the second
normal output. A cache hit consumes no new raw words. These finite-grid
transforms approximate their continuous distribution targets; the grid truncates
extreme tails. Exact raw-engine reproducibility does not imply cross-libm
bitwise equality for log, sine and cosine. The empirical means have theoretical
targets p,mu and1/lambda under their model, but one finite seed is not a
distribution-quality or independence certificate.

> A tiny actual producing history

The table and native concept diagram read the same producing execution. Every
block below is a complete editable C++ program.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/random_generation_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    namespace r = dm::random;
    auto t = r::sample_distributions_trace(42, 2);
    std::print("\n| actual tuple | raw before | after integer | after Bernoulli | after normal | after " "exponential |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.samples.size(); ++i) {
        const auto &s = t.samples[i];
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", i, s.raw_offsets[0],
                   s.raw_offsets[1], s.raw_offsets[2], s.raw_offsets[3],
                   s.raw_offsets[4]);
    }
    const auto &first = t.samples[0];
    const auto &second = t.samples[1];

    g::Diagram d;
    d.node("n0", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual first tuple normal raw calls\n{}",
                       first.raw_offsets[3] - first.raw_offsets[2]));
    d.node("n1", {10, 0},
           std::format("Actual second tuple normal raw calls\n{}",
                       second.raw_offsets[3] - second.raw_offsets[2]));
    d.node("n2", {20, 0},
           std::format("Actual total raw words including uniform\n{}",
                       t.raw_draws));
    d.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    d.save_svg("build/random_generation_tiny.svg");
}
```
