> API and interpretation

The original integer-interval prose described modulo-prefix rejection; the
implementation actually uses Lemire multiply-high rejection, which this report
now states. A native observer delegates to the same distribution methods and
records actual raw words, including rejected candidates and the post-loop
uniform draw. Counting does not substitute a second sampling algorithm. Extreme
finite uniform spans now stay finite, and rounding to an exclusive upper
endpoint clamps inward. Overflowing exponential/normal results throw after
consuming their underlying sampler state. The nominal standard-error curve is a
theoretical scale, not an empirical confidence interval, and finite errors need
not decrease monotonically. None of this makes SplitMix64 a cryptographic
entropy source.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

